---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def, { BRICKS, BRICKS_PER_MORTAR_BAG } from '../calculators/brick';
import { bricksPerFt2 } from '../lib/finish';
import { fmt, int, ceilCount } from '../lib/format';

const table = BRICKS.map((b) => {
  const per = bricksPerFt2(b.l, b.h, 0.375);
  return { label: b.label, per, per100: ceilCount(100 * per) };
});
const walls = [50, 100, 200, 500, 1000].map((a) => {
  const n = ceilCount(a * bricksPerFt2(7.625, 2.25, 0.375) * 1.05);
  return { a, n, bags: ceilCount(n / BRICKS_PER_MORTAR_BAG) };
});
---

<CalcPage
  slug="brick-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="Brick Calculator: How Many Bricks & Mortar Bags You Need"
  description="Free brick calculator. Enter the wall size and brick type to get the number of bricks per square foot, total bricks with waste, and bags of mortar mix."
  h1="Brick Calculator"
  updated="2026-09-26"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Count the bricks and mortar for a wall, planter, or veneer from the wall size, brick size, and mortar joint.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How to calculate bricks</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the wall area in square feet (length × height) and subtract doors and windows.</li>
    <li>Add the mortar joint to the brick's length and height and multiply: that is the face area one brick covers.</li>
    <li>Bricks per square foot = 144 ÷ that area. Multiply by the wall area and add about 5%.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="example">Example: a 20 × 6 ft garden wall in modular brick</h2>
  <p>
    The wall is 120 square feet. A modular brick (7⅝ × 2¼ in) with a ⅜-inch joint covers 8 × 2⅝ = 21 square inches, so
    there are 6.86 bricks per square foot. 120 × 6.86 × 1.05 = <strong>864 bricks</strong>, and about 22 bags of mortar
    mix for a single-wythe wall.
  </p>

  <h2 id="per-sqft">How many bricks per square foot?</h2>
  <p>With a ⅜-inch mortar joint:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Brick</th><th class="num">Bricks per sq ft</th><th class="num">Bricks per 100 sq ft</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{table.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.label}</td><td class="num">{fmt(r.per, 2)}</td><td class="num">{r.per100}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="table">Bricks and mortar for common wall areas</h2>
  <p>Modular brick, ⅜-inch joint, 5% extra, single wythe.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Wall area</th><th class="num">Bricks</th><th class="num">80-lb mortar bags</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{walls.map((w) => <tr><td>{w.a} sq ft</td><td class="num">{int(w.n)}</td><td class="num">{w.bags}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mortar">How much mortar do you need?</h2>
  <p>
    As a planning figure, an 80-lb bag of premixed mortar lays roughly {BRICKS_PER_MORTAR_BAG} standard-size bricks. Wider
    joints, larger bricks, and messy work use more, so check the yield on the bag you buy. Large jobs usually switch to
    masonry cement mixed with sand on site.
  </p>

  <p>
    Garden and retaining walls usually sit on a reinforced footing; <a href="/rebar-calculator/">work out the rebar</a> along with the concrete.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>What is a wythe?</h3>
  <p>
    One vertical layer of brick. Brick veneer on a house is a single wythe; a freestanding structural wall is often two
    wythes, which doubles the brick count.
  </p>
  <h3>How much extra brick should I order?</h3>
  <p>About 5% for breakage and cuts, more if you have many corners or openings to cut around.</p>
  <p>
    Building on a new footing? Size it with the <a href="/concrete-calculator/">concrete calculator</a>. For a patio
    instead of a wall, see the <a href="/paver-calculator/">paver calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Bricks per sq ft = 144 ÷ [(brick length + joint) × (brick height + joint)].</li>
    <li>Bricks = (wall area − openings) × bricks per sq ft × (1 + waste %), rounded up.</li>
    <li>Mortar bags = bricks ÷ {BRICKS_PER_MORTAR_BAG}, rounded up; an estimate that varies with joint size.</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
